Trip Planner architecture plan
==============================

Goal
Build a Vite + React + TypeScript single-page game where the user picks a city, assembles a day itinerary under fixed resource limits, sees live combo/penalty feedback, and finishes on an animated score/results screen.

Module structure
----------------
Use the issue-prescribed top-level folders and keep business logic out of components:

- `src/app/`
  - `App.tsx`: phase router and app theme shell
  - `providers.tsx`: optional test/app providers if needed later
- `src/components/`
  - `city/CityCard.tsx`
  - `hud/ResourceHUD.tsx`
  - `attractions/AttractionCard.tsx`
  - `attractions/AttractionGrid.tsx`
  - `itinerary/YourDayPanel.tsx`
  - `modifiers/ModifiersPanel.tsx`
  - `results/FinishScreen.tsx`
  - `results/TripStyleBadge.tsx`
  - `ui/`: shadcn primitives
- `src/data/`
  - `cities/tokyo.ts`
  - `cities/paris.ts`
  - `cities/new-york.ts`
  - `registry.ts`
- `src/engine/`
  - `types.ts`: domain types and constants
  - `resources.ts`: totals, limit checks, warning thresholds
  - `modifiers.ts`: combo/penalty rules
  - `scoring.ts`: base score, modifier application, trip-style classification
  - `selectors.ts`: pure derived helpers shared by store/tests
- `src/lib/`
  - `cn.ts`
  - `format.ts`
  - `icons.ts` or `theme.ts` if city/category display metadata grows
- `src/pages/`
  - `CitySelectPage.tsx`
  - `GameBoardPage.tsx`
  - `FinishPage.tsx`
- `src/store/`
  - `game-store.ts`: Zustand state/actions/selectors
- `src/test/`
  - `setup.ts`
  - fixtures/helpers for RTL/Playwright

Shared types
------------
Keep all canonical types in `src/engine/types.ts` so data, engine, store, and UI import from one place.

- `Category`: string union for `food | museum | outdoors | nightlife | shopping`
- `CityId`: `tokyo | paris | new-york`
- `Attraction`: id, cityId, name, category, description?, timeHours, costUsd, energy, score, tags?
- `ItineraryItem`: attractionId plus selected order if stable identity is needed
- `Itinerary`: ordered `Attraction[]` or ids plus lookup; prefer ids in store and registry lookup for normalization
- `ResourceTotals`: timeHours, costUsd, energy
- `Modifier`: id, label, kind (`bonus | penalty`), delta, reason
- `TripStyle`: union of the named outcomes from issue 13
- `GamePhase`: `city-select | playing | finished`
- `GameResult`: itinerary, totals, modifiers, score, tripStyle
- constants: `MAX_TIME_HOURS = 12`, `MAX_BUDGET_USD = 150`, `MAX_ENERGY = 100`, warning threshold `0.8`

Key design decisions
--------------------
- Normalize city data around ids. Store selected attraction ids in Zustand, then derive full attraction objects from the selected city registry. This avoids duplicating attraction objects in state and keeps dataset changes localized.
- Keep all rules pure. `resources.ts`, `modifiers.ts`, and `scoring.ts` should be framework-agnostic pure functions so they are easy to unit test and to reuse from components, selectors, and Playwright assertions.
- Make modifiers declarative. Model combo/penalty rules as an array of rule functions or rule definitions returning optional `Modifier` objects instead of hardcoding score math inside the store.
- Centralize derived state. The store should own mutable state and thin actions only; totals, active modifiers, final score, and trip style should be derived through shared selectors/pure functions to prevent drift between live HUD and finish screen.
- Prepare for accessibility/mobile early. Component APIs should expose labels, disabled reasons, and reduced-motion-safe animations so issues 24 and 25 are additive passes rather than rewrites.
- Treat theme metadata as data. City registry entries should carry display data like label, accent color, and optional illustration tokens so `CitySelect`, app shell background, and results can share one source of truth.

Issue dependency map
--------------------
1. Foundation
   - `#1` Vite/TS scaffold unlocks `#2`, `#3`, `#4`, `#5`, `#6`
   - `#2` Tailwind/shadcn provides UI primitives for all visual work
   - `#3` shared runtime deps/utilities support store, animation, icons, formatting
   - `#4` lint/format/env should land before broad implementation
   - `#5` test harness should exist before feature work accumulates
2. Domain and content
   - `#6` types/constants first
   - `#7`, `#8`, `#9` datasets depend on `#6`
   - `#10` city registry depends on all three datasets
3. Game engine
   - `#11` resource validation depends on `#6`
   - `#12` modifier engine depends on `#6`
   - `#13` scoring/classifier depends on `#11` and `#12`
   - `#14` Zustand store depends on `#10`, `#11`, `#12`, `#13`
4. UI flow
   - `#15` app shell/phase router depends on `#14`
   - `#16` city select depends on `#15` plus registry theme metadata
   - `#17` HUD depends on store selectors/resource thresholds
   - `#18` attraction card depends on `canAdd`, formatting, icons
   - `#19` attraction grid depends on `#18`
   - `#20` itinerary panel depends on store/remove/finish actions
   - `#21` modifiers panel depends on live derived modifiers
   - `#22` finish screen depends on final score/result derivations
   - `#23` trip-style badge depends on classifier and finish screen
5. Polish and verification
   - `#24` mobile layout depends on completed board composition
   - `#25` accessibility depends on most interactive UI existing
   - `#26` component tests depend on HUD/card/panel stability
   - `#27` happy-path E2E depends on end-to-end flow being complete
   - `#28` edge-case E2E depends on validation and modifier behavior being complete
   - `#29` deploy README depends on verified production build and env/docs

Recommended implementation sequence
-----------------------------------
Follow the numerical order with one practical adjustment: finish `#6`, `#11`, `#12`, `#13`, and `#10` before `#14`, even if dataset authoring happens in parallel. That yields a stable engine/store contract before any major UI work.

Risk areas to watch
-------------------
- Dataset balance can invalidate combo/penalty and classifier expectations; keep dataset review explicit when issues `#7` to `#10` land.
- If the store holds full attraction objects instead of ids, later refactors around filters/theme data become noisier.
- Disabled-state UX is critical for tests and accessibility; standardize a single `reason` enum/string contract from `canAdd`.
- Finish-screen score breakdown should reuse the same modifier data structure used live to avoid inconsistent totals.
